The City's Comprehensive Integrated Transport Plan (CITP) 2023 - 2028 highlights the role and importance of Travel Demand Management (TDM) in addition to infrastructural improvements to improve access and mobility in Cape Town. The City's TDM Strategy was approved in 2017 and identified a range of measures to reduce the demand for private vehicle travel, and to contribute to managing congestion and emissions by reducing single-occupancy car use, especially during peak periods. 

Since 2017, there have been many contextual changes that have taken place, which led to the need for a review and revision of the current Strategy. These include:
.-The City's approach to policy and strategy development has evolved to be vision-led; demonstrate the Theory of Change; and have strong implementation plans and monitoring and evaluation (M&E) plans;
-An increased uptake in technology/software supporting digital access;
-An increase in car ownership and private transport use;
-The requirement for an evaluation review to monitor implementation progress; and 
-Ensure emerging priorities around the climate change and resilience are reflected in the low-carbon mobility response through the TDM Strategy

A detailed evaluation of the 2017 TDM Strategy was undertaken and a revised Strategy was drafted. The overall aim is for private vehicle travel to be shifted to public transport, walking, cycling & lift clubbing; and avoided by substituting with digital access, where appropriate. The Strategy has identified a number of key categories, within which various TDM measures fall, these include: voluntary; institutional; infrastructure; regulatory and pricing. The draft Strategy will soon be available to the public for comment through the Public Participation Process and Council approval process is expected to be complete in early 2026.

The City of Cape Town approved its new Walking and Cycling Strategy in April 2025 to enable the shift to active mobility, not only for the full journey, but also as the first and last mile to public transport. The updated strategy replaces the Cycling Strategy (2017) and the City's previous NMT Strategy (draft, 2017), and supports and aligns with the Integrated Development Plan (IDP), Comprehensive Integrated Transport Plan (CITP), Climate Change Strategy as well as the recently updated Universal Access Policy. 

The Strategy aims to reposition walking and cycling as primary, safe, accessible, and sustainable modes of travel in Cape Town. In light of this, a more positive collective term, 'active mobility', was adopted by the City to replace the term 'non-motorised transport' (NMT). The Strategy will enable the City to implement measures that make walking and cycling safer, more accessible and more enjoyable for all people in Cape Town. The Strategy proposes four key focus areas, namely:
a)	Targeted investment through incrementalism; 
b)	Governance, funding, and collaboration; 
c)	Mainstreaming pedestrians and cyclists in infrastructure design and management; and 
d)	Community building and demand-side interventions.

Msunduzi Environmental Management Framework (2010)

The Msunduzi EMF (2010) on page 34 states the following:

'The Msunduzi EMF should follow a formal review process every 5 years. The review should use the updated data to:

•	Inform the preparation of review reports;
•	Update the desired state of the environment based on a public participation process; 
•	Amend the management priorities and desirable and undesirable activities in terms of the environmental management zones.'

In compliance with the above, the Msunduzi EMF is now required to be reviewed and updated.

Background of the Msunduzi EMF (2010)

The Msunduzi EMF aims to identify opportunities and constraints that will guide site specific studies such as Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA) as well as municipal scale planning. 

The broad objectives of the EMF were to:

•	Identify areas both suitable and unsuitable for development;
•	Provide information to assist decision making (such as development applications) and thereby streamline the process;
•	Identify environmentally sensitive areas that require protection so as to ensure ecosystem service delivery and the achievement of local municipal biodiversity conservation targets;
•	Provide environmental goals and mechanisms to achieve the objectives as stated.

These objectives have been achieved through the:

•	Identification and description of the environmental attributes or characteristics of Msunduzi Municipality; 
•	Creation of a consolidated development sensitivity map (CEAM layer);
•	Creation of an interactive software interface (using GIS software) that allows for reporting on nine attributes based on their geographical locations (SDST); 
•	Identification of management priorities for each attribute: 
•	Identification of listed activities that may be triggered by development within areas where the environmental attributes exist; and 
•	Identification of the preferred and non-preferred land use where environmental attributes exist.

Below are the nine environmental control zones or attributes that were identified within the Msunduzi Municipality to inform the EMF and the associated CEAM layer and SDST:

•	Wetland Areas
•	Areas of biodiversity significance (specifically Critical Biodiversity Areas and Ecological support Areas), Corridor linkages must also be highlighted;
•	Areas of flood risk
•	Areas of high agricultural potential
•	Areas with steep slopes
•	Areas of water quality constraint
•	Areas of Air Quality constraint
•	Areas of cult

